figdown 0.1 block
# `DRAWN-ANNOTATION-FORM`: the OTHER half of the compatibility promise. `note=`
# moved the language number 0.2 → 0.3, and a version bump is only affordable
# if documents written against the older numbers keep parsing exactly as they
# did. This file is the assertion that they do.
#
# WHAT COULD HAVE GONE WRONG, which is why this is not an empty test. The
# release touched four shared mechanisms: `LANG_VERSIONS` gained a member,
# `GENRES_BY_VERSION` gained a row, `OPT_MIN_VERSION`/`belowOptVersion` are
# consulted on every option key, and `RETIRED_OPT_KEYS` LOST its `note` row.
# The last one is the sharpest: removing a retired-key row is exactly the
# kind of edit that takes a neighbouring row with it. So the sections below
# do not merely avoid `note=` — they exercise the machinery around it.
#
#   Section 1 (`figdown 0.1`): a 0.1 document using `description=`, the key
#     `note=` was renamed TO. It still means what it always meant — an SVG
#     <title>, no ink — and its acceptor is still `field`.
#   Section 2 (`figdown 0.2`): a 0.2 document using the 0.2 vocabulary that
#     `figdown 0.1` refuses (`flowline`, `KEYWORD-RENAME-SCOPE`). The 0.2 gate is unmoved by
#     the arrival of a third version.
#
# Neither section mentions `note=` anywhere. That is the point: a document
# that never heard of the key must not be able to tell that the key exists.
group ring "Access ring"
node a "A" in=ring
node b "B" in=ring
edge a -[10G]- b
bitfield hdr "Header" word=16 numbering=msb0
field "Type" 4 description="prose a machine reads — still the live spelling, still field-only"
field "Len" 12

figdown 0.2 flowchart
# Section 2: the 0.2-only vocabulary, still gated exactly as `KEYWORD-RENAME-SCOPE` left it.
process p "Classify"
terminator t "Done"
flowline p -> t
